  "query_preview": "../Tuesday through Friday/...An active pattern continues \ninto the beginning of the week as a low redevelops in the central \nGulf, continuing to bring waves of precipitation with showery \nonshore flow between them lasting through midweek. The low appears \nto redevelop around 140W early Tuesday morning, looking notably less \nstrong than the current system in the Gulf that impacted the area \nthis weekend. It is expected to bring another more organized wave of \nprecipitation northward across the panhandle during the day Tuesday, \nfollowed by a few less organized and showery waves Wednesday into \nThursday. This midweek low looks to be a bit more aligned today \nbetween models on the main precipitation band moving through \nTuesday, with all of them appearing to shift the low and front \nsouthward a bit compared to yesterday. Expecting much of the \nrainfall Tuesday to impact the southern panhandle at this time, with \nsome areas around Ketchikan and Metlakatla seeing potential for up \nto 1 to 1.5 inches in 24 hours as the main band moves through. Some \nuncertainty still remains on timing and rainfall amounts, especially \ndue to the more showery regime expected midweek and the spread on \nthe low's track/location especially moving into Wednesday. Some \nguidance shows the low remaining further west, while some have it \nmoving in towards the panhandle with the low center sitting east of \n140W during the day Tuesday. This will also bring some lower \nconfidence in some of the marine winds, depending on how far \noffshore or how close to the panhandle the main low center ends up \nmoving to.",
